A few years ago, I began an update to MIT{}'s \LaTeX{} template for
theses and dissertations. The previous template had been written in
\LaTeX{} 2.09 in the 1980s and patched for \LaTeXe{} by various students
during the 1990s. My work quickly turned into a complete rewrite. My
goals were to use current \LaTeX{} methods and to produce tagged
\acro{PDF} while still supporting users who have minimal knowledge of
\LaTeX{} and typesetting. In this note, I discuss some of
the considerations that have shaped the current template, the
implementation of tagging for non-standard document elements, rendering
in \acro{HTML}/\acro{CSS}, and some aspects of \tbcode{expl3} coding.

